1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:05,428
Welcome to Episode Nine, an extended lesson on the Judas Swing.

2
00:00:05,848 --> 00:00:15,664
We will build two fully annotated cases, one bullish and one bearish, and then study the situations where the right decision is not to force the label at all.

3
00:00:17,484 --> 00:00:24,708
The bullish case. First, the higher timeframe: higher highs and higher lows, so structure is bullish.

4
00:00:25,128 --> 00:00:32,280
The draw is buy-side liquidity above the old high, and price is in discount. The Daily Bias is bullish.

5
00:00:32,700 --> 00:00:37,236
On the thirty-minute chart, mark the midnight open and the Asian range.

6
00:00:37,656 --> 00:00:43,800
Relevant liquidity: sell-side below the Asian low, and buy-side above the previous day high.

7
00:00:44,220 --> 00:00:49,188
Session timing: the London Kill Zone, two to five a m New York.

8
00:00:49,608 --> 00:00:55,872
Everything so far is defined before London opens. That is what makes the next move interpretable.

9
00:00:57,692 --> 00:01:06,044
London opens. Price initially moves down, below the midnight open, and sweeps sell-side liquidity under the Asian low.

10
00:01:06,464 --> 00:01:10,808
The down move fails to sustain. The next candle closes back up.

11
00:01:11,228 --> 00:01:17,468
Bullish displacement follows, leaving a fair value gap, where present, as a PD array.

12
00:01:17,888 --> 00:01:22,424
Price closes above the last lower high: a market structure shift.

13
00:01:22,844 --> 00:01:31,532
In New York, price reaches buy-side liquidity above the previous day high. Ten annotations, all visible, all in order.

14
00:01:33,352 --> 00:01:44,128
The bearish case mirrors it. Higher-timeframe structure is bearish: lower highs, lower lows, price in premium, and the draw is sell-side liquidity below.

15
00:01:44,548 --> 00:01:51,772
Before London, mark the midnight open, the Asian high with buy-side liquidity above it, and the previous day low.

16
00:01:52,192 --> 00:01:58,600
London opens with an initial move up, above the midnight open, sweeping buy-side liquidity.

17
00:01:59,020 --> 00:02:06,964
Bearish displacement follows, leaving a bearish fair value gap, and a close below the last higher low shifts structure.

18
00:02:07,384 --> 00:02:11,896
Price then moves toward sell-side liquidity below the previous day low.

19
00:02:12,316 --> 00:02:14,884
Same ten annotations, inverted.

20
00:02:16,704 --> 00:02:22,992
No-trade scenario one: the sweep occurs, but no displacement follows. Price drifts.

21
00:02:23,412 --> 00:02:27,804
Scenario two: the market continues straight through the swept level.

22
00:02:28,224 --> 00:02:35,544
Scenario three: price bounces but fails to establish a meaningful structure. The lower high holds.

23
00:02:35,964 --> 00:02:41,460
In all three, the correct decision is not to force a Judas Swing interpretation.

24
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:52,112
Scenario four: the Daily Bias is unclear. Price sits in the middle of a range, between equal highs and equal lows.

25
00:02:52,532 --> 00:02:59,564
Without a bias, an initial move cannot be counter to anything. There is no Judas Swing to identify.

26
00:02:59,984 --> 00:03:07,736
Scenario five: session timing is not relevant. Here the Asian low is swept at noon New York time, during lunch.

27
00:03:08,156 --> 00:03:14,492
That is a sweep, but it is outside the session-open context in which the Judas Swing is defined.

28
00:03:14,912 --> 00:03:20,072
In both cases, the professional answer is to avoid forcing the interpretation.

29
00:03:21,892 --> 00:03:31,444
Here is the decision table. Only when bias, timing, sweep, displacement and structure all line up is the interpretation supported.

30
00:03:31,864 --> 00:03:38,272
A sweep without displacement, or a continuation through the level, is not supported or is rejected.

31
00:03:38,692 --> 00:03:43,996
An unclear bias or irrelevant timing makes the concept not applicable.

32
00:03:44,416 --> 00:03:50,008
And no structure shift means no support. When in doubt, the decision is no trade.

33
00:03:51,828 --> 00:04:02,916
To summarise. Context comes first: higher timeframe, bias, liquidity and timing. Then the initial move, the sweep, displacement and structure.

34
00:04:03,336 --> 00:04:09,792
A fair value gap is optional confluence, and the objective is liquidity on the opposite side.

35
00:04:10,212 --> 00:04:15,420
Remember the five no-trade scenarios. No interpretation is a valid result.

36
00:04:17,240 --> 00:04:22,976
Time to check your understanding. Pause the video and answer these five questions in your own words.

37
00:04:24,896 --> 00:04:30,320
Write down your reasoning, not just a one-word answer. The reasoning is what we assess.

38
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:40,356
Here are the short answers. Compare them with your reasoning; the workbook explains each one in full.

39
